.\" sash.1 - DRAFT man page for every switch-taking surface in safebash.
.\" Every behavior below was verified empirically against
.\" target/release/sash on 2026-09-08, not read off the source comments.
.TH SASH 1 "2026-09-08" "safebash" "General Commands Manual"
.
.SH NAME
sash \- compile a safe subset of bash to a static native binary
.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sash
.RI [ options ]
.I script.sh
.RB [ \-o
.IR output ]
.br
.B sash
.B \-c
.I 'script'
.RB [ \-o
.IR output ]
.br
.B sash
.B \-\-extract
.I binary
.br
.B sash
.B \-\-help
.
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B sash
compiles a script written in the safe subset of bash to a static ELF
binary. The subset is defined by safebash's own design decisions, not by
.BR bash (1)
compatibility: where the two differ,
.B sash
implements the behaviour its design decisions specify.
.PP
This page documents every surface in safebash that takes a switch: the
compiler driver, the three builtins that accept flags
.RB ( read ,
.BR mapfile ,
.BR echo ),
the
.RB \[dq] [[ \ ]] \[dq]
test operators that share the same
.BI \- X
namespace, and
.BR sash\-convert (1).
.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Input and output
.TP
.BI \-c \ script
Compile
.I script
given inline instead of reading a file.
.TP
.BI \-o \ output
Write the binary to
.IR output .
.TP
.B \-h ", " \-\-help
Print the usage summary on standard output and exit 0.
.
.SS Alternative backends
Each writes to standard output and exits; no binary is produced.
.TP
.B \-\-emit\-ir
Emit the IR as an inert Janet data literal \(em data, not a program.
Intended for out-of-tree consumers such as the decoupled oracle
(DD#104); the leading
.B :ir\-version
key lets a consumer refuse a capture it does not understand.
.TP
.B \-\-emit\-c
Emit C source. Link against
.B libsash_rt.a
.RB ( SASH_RT_LIB ).
.TP
.B \-\-manifest
Emit the manifest (external dependencies).
.
.SS Inspection
.TP
.BI \-\-extract \ binary
Dump the
.B .sash_source
ELF section of a compiled
.IR binary .
The binary must follow the flag directly
.RB ( sash \ \-\-extract \ BIN );
it is read positionally.
.TP
.B \-\-trace
Enable tracing in the emitted binary.
Native backend only, and opt\-in twice over: the flag compiles the trace
sites in, and
.B SASH_TRACE=1
activates them at run time. A binary built without it ignores the
variable.
.TP
.BI \-\-heap\-size= size
Set the runtime heap reservation, as a byte count with an optional
.BR K ,
.BR M ,
or
.B G
suffix \(em for example
.BR 512K ,
.BR 64M ,
.BR 2G .
Default 256M (DD#107).
.
The heap is a single fixed region, so this is a hard ceiling rather than a
starting size; exceeding it is a clean
.B sash: out of memory
diagnostic with a non\-zero status, never a segfault. The region is mapped
.BR MAP_NORESERVE ,
so a large reservation costs address space rather than memory.
.
The size is compiled into the binary and is deliberately
.B not
readable from the environment: there is no
.BR $SASH_HEAP .
.
.SS Taint bypasses
See DD#82. These weaken the guarantee that
.B sash
exists to provide; use them deliberately.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-taint\-cst
.B A testing aid only \(em not for general use.
Disable the front-end (CST) taint pass only. The SSA taint backstop still
runs, which is how the backstop is exercised in isolation. Deliberately
absent from
.BR "sash \-\-help" :
it exists to test the compiler, not to be reached for while writing
programs. Use
.B \-\-no\-taint
if you genuinely need a bypass.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-taint
Disable both taint passes.
.
.SS Environment
.TP
.BI \-\-sysroot= path
Set the sysroot used for command resolution (DD#23). A directory or a
.B .squashfs
/
.B .sqsh
image. Every command path \(em whether found by the PATH search or written
out in full \(em is checked against this root rather than the build host.
A root that cannot be read \(em a missing directory, or an image
.B unsquashfs
cannot open \(em is a usage error reported before compilation starts, not a
per\-command diagnostic.
.PP
A command path must name a
.B regular file with an execute bit
in that root. A bare name that matches nothing executable on PATH is
.BR SB5011 ;
a relative path is
.BR SB5051 ;
an absolute path that is absent, a directory, a device node, or not
executable is
.BR SB5095 ,
and the message names which. Use
.RB \[dq] "[[ \-x /path ]]" \[dq]
to test for a program at
.I run
time, and
.B /usr/bin/env
to defer the search to one.
.PP
A command name is a literal path. There is no globbing in command position:
.B *
is a glob only in an array context (DD#39), so
.B /bin/ech*
names a program that does not exist rather than matching one.
.PP
A command name whose text has no bare spelling \(em most often a path
containing a space \(em may be written in
.B single
quotes, which denote the literal name:
.RB \[dq] "\[aq]/opt/my tool\[aq]" \[dq] .
Every quoted
.I constant
name is judged at parse time, by whichever rule is true of it.
Quoting a name that could have been written bare is
.BR SB5096 ;
so is a name that genuinely needs quoting but is said some other way \(em
double\-quoted
.RB ( \[dq]/opt/my\ tool\[dq] ),
or assembled from several quoted and bare pieces \(em with the opposite
advice.
One rule earns one code: a command name is one bare word, or one
single\-quoted word when it has no bare spelling.
An empty name
.RB ( \[aq]\[aq] " or " \[dq]\[dq] )
is
.BR SB4074 :
a zero\-length name can denote neither an intrinsic nor a program, so it is a
syntax error rather than a resolution failure.
.PP
.B SB5011
is left for a head that really is resolved at runtime \(em one containing a
variable, an expansion or a capture \(em which is what its text has always
said.
.
.SS Rejected here, accepted by sash\-convert
.B \-\-emit\-source ,
.B \-\-annotate ,
and
.B \-\-nolint
are rejected with a message directing you to
.BR sash\-convert (1).
Source transformation belongs to the converter, not the compiler (DD#57).
.
.SH BUILTINS THAT TAKE FLAGS
.SS read
.B read
.RB [ \-a
.IR array ]
.RB [ \-d
.IR delim ]
.RB [ \-f
.IR sep ]
.RB [ \-r ]
.RI [ name ...]
.PP
A byte-valued flag may be joined
.RB ( \-d: )
or split
.RB ( \-d\ : ).
An empty value
.RB ( \-d\ '' ,
or a bare trailing
.BR \-d )
means NUL, following the bash
.B read -d ''
convention.
.TP
.BI \-a \ array
Read into
.IR array .
See BUGS: the split here uses
.BR \-d ,
not
.BR \-f .
.TP
.BI \-d \ delim
Record delimiter \(em the byte that
.B terminates the line.
Reading stops there. This is
.B not
the field separator.
.TP
.BI \-f \ sep
Field separator \(em the byte that separates fields
.I within
the line. This is bash's
.B IFS
for a single command.
.TP
.B \-r
Accepted and ignored. sash reads are always raw, so
.B \-r
requests the default.
.PP
Any other
.BI \- flag
is rejected with
.BR SB5015 .
.PP
With one target, the whole line is assigned. With several, each target but
the last takes one field and
.B the last takes the remainder:
.PP
.RS
.EX
echo "a b c d" | read x y      # x=a  y="b c d"
echo "a:b:c:d" | read -f: x y  # x=a  y="b:c:d"
echo "a:b:c:d" | read -d: x y  # x=a  y=""      (line ends at the first ':')
.EE
.RE
.PP
.B read
targets are tainted (DD#24); reading one requires
.BR attest .
Use
.B _
to bind and discard a field you do not want (DD#98).
.
.SS mapfile
.B mapfile
.RB [ \-m ]
.RB [ \-f c ]
.RB [ \-t ]
.I target
.TP
.B \-m
Map mode: read
.IB key sep value
lines into a map rather than an array.
.TP
.BI \-f c
The map key/value separator, default
.BR = .
Joined
.RB ( \-f: )
or split
.RB ( \-f\ : ).
.TP
.B \-t
Accepted and ignored. sash always strips the trailing newline.
.PP
.B readarray
is
.B not
supported \(em it is rejected by command resolution
.RB ( SB5011 ).
Use
.BR mapfile .
.PP
Unknown flags are
.B silently ignored
here. See BUGS.
.
.SS echo
.B echo
.RB [ \-n ]
.RI [ arg ...]
.TP
.B \-n
Suppress the trailing newline. Recognized
.B only as the first argument;
elsewhere
.B \-n
is an ordinary word and is printed.
.PP
There is no
.BR \-e .
sash does not interpret backslash escapes in
.BR echo ;
.B \-e
is printed literally, as is the text after it.
.
.SH REDIRECTION
A redirect may attach only to something that produces output or consumes
input. That is
.BR echo ,
.BR write ,
.BR exec ,
.BR read ,
.BR readline ,
an external command, a user function, or a pipeline \[em] plus
.BR : ,
which is a deliberate exception described below.
.PP
Every other builtin writes to no descriptor, so a redirect on it can only
create and truncate the file as a side effect. bash does exactly that; sash
rejects it with
.B SB4046
rather than copy the wart:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
cd /tmp > f                  # SB4046
exit 0 > f                   # SB4046
close 9 > f                  # SB4046
unset m[k] > f               # SB4046
argv 1 a > f                 # SB4046
match \[dq]$s\[dq] /a/ > f           # SB4046
attest s [[ \-n $s ]] > f     # SB4046
return 0 > f                 # SB4046
.fi
.RE
.PP
The same rule covers a value\-producing intrinsic. These are read as a
capture \[em]
.BR getenv ,
.BR arg ,
.BR argc ,
.BR status ,
.BR epochseconds ,
.BR random ,
.BR getpid ,
.BR getppid ,
.BR getcpid ,
.BR waitstatus ,
.BR getcwd ,
.BR signum
\[em] and a capture has no descriptor to redirect, so
.B \[dq]$(getcwd 2>/dev/null)\[dq]
is
.BR SB4046 .
.PP
The same rule covers a function definition, a variable declaration, a bare
assignment, an arithmetic test and a
.RB \[dq] [[ \ ]] \[dq]
test.
.PP
.B \[dq]:\[dq]
is the exception. It produces no output either, but
.B \[dq]: > f\[dq]
is the universal spelling for truncating a file, so it honours its redirects
and creates or truncates the target.
.PP
.B return
follows the shape of its operand.
.B \[dq]return N\[dq]
names the status outright and is rejected above;
.B \[dq]return CMD\[dq]
derives the status from a producer that does produce output, so
.B \[dq]return /bin/true 2>/dev/null\[dq]
is accepted and the redirect applies to the producer (DD#74).
.PP
.B \[dq]exec CMD > f\[dq]
installs the redirect and then replaces the process, so
.B CMD
inherits it \[em] bash's behaviour. The no\-argument form
.B \[dq]exec > f\[dq]
rebinds the invoker's own descriptors for the rest of the program, and is the
one redirect form that also moves the diagnostic channel (DD#120).
.PP
A redirect says where output goes; it does not dispose of the exit status.
.B \[dq]cmd > f\[dq]
sets
.B $?
exactly as
.B cmd
does. Suppression is the one wrapper that does dispose of it:
.B \[dq]\- cmd\[dq]
elects the
.I ignored
disposition (DD#21), so reading
.B $?
afterwards is
.BR SB5012 .
.
.SH TEST OPERATORS
The
.RB \[dq] [[ \ ]] \[dq]
operators occupy the same
.BI \- X
namespace as the flags above but are
.B operators, not switches.
This is the most common source of confusion \(em see BUGS.
.SS Unary
.TP
.B \-e \-f \-d \-s \-r \-w \-x \-h \-L \-b \-c \-p \-S \-t \-O \-G \-N \-a
File and descriptor tests, as in
.BR bash (1).
.TP
.B \-n \-z
Non-empty / empty string.
.TP
.B \-v \-o \-R
Parsed but
.B rejected
.RB ( SB5028 ),
each with a hint:
.B \-v
\(em use
.B \-n
instead, since under DD#62 an unset or empty variable is just the empty
string;
.B \-o
\(em sash has no shell options;
.B \-R
\(em sash has no namerefs.
.SS Content shape (DD#93)
.TP
.B i? p? g?
Integer-representable / all-printable / all-graph. These are
.B prefix
operators and carry no dash:
.RB \[dq] [[\ i?\ $x\ ]] \[dq].
They also have an
.B attest
shorthand:
.RB \[dq] attest\ x\ p? \[dq].
The
.B ?
suffix keeps them out of bash's saturated
.BI \- X
namespace.
.SS Numeric comparison
.B \-eq \-ne \-lt \-le \-gt \-ge
are
.B rejected
.RB ( SB5019 ).
Arithmetic comparison belongs in
.RB \[dq] ((\ )) \[dq]:
write
.RB \[dq] ((\ a\ ==\ b\ )) \[dq],
not
.RB \[dq] [[\ $a\ \-eq\ $b\ ]] \[dq].
.SS String comparison (DD#118)
.TP
.B ==  !=
String equality and inequality.
.B A single
.B =
is
.B rejected
.RB ( SB4067 ):
it always
.BR assigns ,
so
.RB \[dq] [[\ $x\ =\ y\ ]] \[dq]
would read as a comparison while
.RB \[dq] [[\ $x=y\ ]] \[dq]
is one word. Write
.B ==
for equality, everywhere.
.TP
.B <  >
Byte-order comparison, via the same locale-free compare the runtime uses.
.SS No implicit \-n (DD#118)
A bare word is
.B not
a test
.RB ( SB4068 ).
bash reads
.RB \[dq] [[\ word\ ]] \[dq]
as
.RB \[dq] [[\ \-n\ word\ ]] \[dq];
sash requires the
.B \-n
(or
.BR \-z )
to be written. This is what makes
.RB \[dq] [[\ $x=y\ ]] \[dq]
an error rather than a silently constant
.BR true .
.SS Vacuous tests (DD#118)
A test whose answer is fixed by the source text is
.B rejected
.RB ( SB5086 ):
.RB \[dq] [[\ \-n\ foo\ ]] \[dq],
.RB \[dq] [[\ \-z\ \[dq]\[dq]\ ]] \[dq],
.RB \[dq] [[\ foo\ ==\ foo\ ]] \[dq],
and a
.RB \[dq] [[\ subj\ pat\ {\ }\ ]] \[dq]
dispatch whose subject and patterns are all literal.
The rule is
.BR syntactic :
it stops at the first variable and never constant-folds, so the mechanical
escape always works \(em
.RB \[dq] str\ s=foo;\ [[\ \-n\ $s\ ]] \[dq].
File tests are exempt (a static operand, a runtime answer), and so are the
numeric operators, so that
.B SB5086
cannot shadow
.BR SB5019 .
.
.SS Glued operators need a \[dq]$\[dq] (DD#118)
A comparison written without spaces is recognised only when the left operand
.B starts with an expansion
\(em
.RB \[dq] $x==a \[dq],
.RB \[dq] $x$y==ab \[dq],
.RB \[dq] ${m[$k]}==a \[dq].
A word beginning with static text
.RB ( \[dq] ab==$x \[dq],
.RB \[dq] a$x==ab \[dq],
.RB \[dq] [[a==a]] \[dq])
is
.B rejected
.RB ( SB4069 ).
Bash reads such a word as a bare word, so accepting a glued operator there
answered differently from bash without saying so.
Since
.B ==
commutes, the diagnostic offers both the spaced form
.RB ( \[dq] ab\ ==\ $x \[dq])
and the swap
.RB ( \[dq] $x==ab \[dq]);
.B =~
is offered only the spaced form, because its subject must stay on the left.
.
.SH STRING ESCAPES
Backslash is interpreted in
.B exactly two
places (DD#64): inside
.RB \[dq] $'\|.\|.\|.' \[dq]
and as a line continuation at end of line.
Everywhere else \(em unquoted,
.RB \[dq] '\|.\|.\|.' \[dq]
and
.RB \[dq] \[rs]"\|.\|.\|.\[rs]" \[dq]
\(em a backslash is a verbatim byte.
There is no
.B \[rs]"
or
.B \[rs]$
inside double quotes; escape by quoting and concatenation instead.
.PP
.RB \[dq] $'\|.\|.\|.' \[dq]
works anywhere a
.B $
form does, including inside
.RB \[dq] \[rs]"\|.\|.\|.\[rs]" \[dq].
The escape set is fixed by DD#115.
.SS Accepted
.TP
.B \[rs]n \[rs]t \[rs]r \[rs]a \[rs]b \[rs]e \[rs]E \[rs]f \[rs]v \[rs]\[rs] \[rs]' \[rs]"
The named escapes, as in
.BR bash (1).
.TP
.BI \[rs]x NN
One or two hex digits naming a
.BR byte ,
capped at
.BR \[rs]x7f .
.TP
.BI \[rs]u HHHH \fR,\fP \[rs]U HHHHHHHH
Up to four or eight hex digits naming a
.BR character ,
emitted as UTF\-8.
.TP
.B \[rs]q \[rs]z \fR\(em anything bash does not read as an escape\fP
Stays a literal backslash and byte, exactly as bash leaves it.
.SS Rejected with SB4063
Never silently \(em each names its replacement.
.TP
.B octal
.BR \[rs]0 ,
.BR \[rs]012 ,
.BR \[rs]101 ,
.BR \[rs]777 .
bash reads
.BI \[rs] nnn
as one to
.B three digits total,
so
.B \[rs]0101
is
.B \[rs]010
then
.BR 1 ,
which is easy to misread and which sash got wrong.
Write the byte as
.BI \[rs]x NN
or the character as
.BI \[rs]u HHHH .
.B \[rs]0
is included:
.B \[rs]0
.I is
the octal escape, so a NUL is
.BR $'\[rs]x00' .
.TP
.BI \[rs]c X
Archaic control notation.
.B \[rs]cA
is
.BR \[rs]x01 .
.TP
.BI \[rs]x NN \fP\ above\ \fB\[rs]x7f
A lone byte above ASCII is not UTF\-8 text.
Write the character \(em type it in the UTF\-8 source, or use
.BI \[rs]u HHHH .
.TP
.B a prefix with no digits
.B \[rs]x
and
.BR \[rs]u ,
as DD#72 rejects a bare
.BR 0x .
.TP
.BI \[rs]u HHHH\fP\ that\ is\ not\ a\ character
A surrogate
.RB ( D800 \- DFFF )
or above
.BR 10FFFF .
bash encodes these anyway and emits invalid UTF\-8.
.PP
The cap is a
.B source-literal
constraint only.
The runtime is byte\-clean:
.BR read ,
.BR $(arg\ N) ,
.BR $(getenv\ NAME) ,
.RB \[dq] $(\|.\|.\|.) \[dq]
capture and external passthrough all carry arbitrary bytes intact.
What is given up is authoring a lone byte \(>= 0x80
.I inline.
.
.SH SPECIAL VALUES
Three values that bash spells with punctuation have keyword names in sash,
and the keyword name is the canonical one (DD#75).
The punctuation is accepted as input sugar; it is what
.B sash\-convert
rewrites away.
.TP
.B $(status)
The exit status of the most recent command.
bash spells this
.BR $? .
.TP
.B $(getpid)
This process's pid.
bash spells this
.BR $$ .
.TP
.B $(getcpid)
The pid of the job backgrounded by the immediately preceding statement.
bash spells this
.BR $! .
.TP
.B $(waitstatus)
The exit status of the child reaped by the immediately preceding
.BR wait ,
or
.B \-1
if it reaped nothing.
.PP
.B $(getcpid)
and
.B $(waitstatus)
read single-slot latches that the next statement overwrites without warning,
so each is readable only in the statement
.I immediately following
the one that wrote it
.RB ( SB5094 ).
.PP
.B The spelling depends on where the value sits.
In command position the keyword is a command substitution,
.BR $(status) .
As an arithmetic operand it is the
.RB \[dq] (name\ args\|.\|.\|.) \[dq]
call form,
.BR (status) ,
with no
.BR $ :
.PP
.RS
.EX
echo $(status)              # command position
n=$(( (status) + 1 ))       # arithmetic operand
.EE
.RE
.PP
These are not interchangeable.
.B $(status)
inside
.RB \[dq] $((\ )) \[dq]
is
.BR SB4020 ,
because a command substitution is not an arithmetic operand.
The punctuation sugar
.RB ( $? )
reads the same in both, which is why a mechanical rewrite has to know
which context it is in.
.PP
.B $(status)
is rejected with
.B SB5012
where no command has run yet \(em at the top of a script, and after a
background job, which does not set it.
The punctuation spelling is rejected in exactly the same places.
.
.SH FUNCTIONS AND RECURSION
Functions are
.B inlined.
There is no runtime call stack, so in general a function cannot call itself \[em]
inlining would not terminate \[em] and such calls are rejected with
.BR SB5037 .
.PP
.B One shape is allowed:
a direct self
.I tail
call, where the recursive call is the producer of a
.BR return .
That is compiled to a loop and runs in constant stack.
Write recursion in accumulator form:
.PP
.EX
fn fact \-> int {
  int n=$(($1)); int acc=$(($2))
  if (( n <= 1 )); then return $(( acc )); fi
  int n1=$(( n \- 1 )); int a1=$(( n * acc ))
  return $(( (fact n1 a1) ))
}
int r=$(( (fact 5 1) ))          # 120
.EE
.PP
Everything else is
.BR SB5037 :
.TP
.B non\-tail self\-call
.RB \[dq] return\ $((\ n\ *\ (f\ m)\ ))\[dq]
\[em] the multiply happens
.I after
the call returns, so there is nothing to turn into a loop.
This is what a direct translation of the textbook factorial produces.
.TP
.B mutual recursion
.I f
calls
.IR g ,
which calls
.IR f .
.SH REGULAR EXPRESSIONS
Regexes appear in
.RB \[dq] "[[ $v =~ RE ]]" \[dq],
in the
.BR match ,
.BR sub ,
and
.B gsub
builtins, and in the
.RB \[dq] s/RE/SUB/ \[dq]
sugar.
.PP
The engine is
.BR "Rob Pike's Plan\ 9 regexp" ,
used deliberately. It is a Thompson NFA: no backtracking, so match time is
linear in the subject and no pattern can be made to blow up exponentially.
.B POSIX compliance is not a goal.
A handful of additions have been made for convenience, not for conformance.
Expect a close relative of bash's
.BR =~ ,
not a drop\-in replacement; the differences are listed below and are
intentional.
.SS Core
.TP
.B . * + ? | ( ) ^ $
As in POSIX ERE.
.B .
matches any byte except newline;
.B ^
and
.B $
anchor at the subject's start and end.
.TP
.B [abc] [a\-c] [^abc]
Bracket expressions, with ranges and negation.
.TP
.B \e.
A backslash makes any metacharacter literal.
.SS Additions
These are
.B not
in POSIX ERE and are
.B not
accepted by
.BR bash (1).
.TP
.BI { n } "\fR, \fP" { n , m } "\fR, \fP" { n ,}
Counted repetition of the preceding atom. Each bound is at most
.BR 32 ;
a larger one is rejected
.RB ( "repetition count too large" ).
The open form
.BI { n ,}
is unbounded above.
.TP
.B \ed \eD \ew \eW \es \eS
Digit, word, and whitespace classes, and their complements.
.TP
.BI \ex NN
The byte with hex value
.IR NN ,
exactly two digits. Lets a pattern hold any literal byte \(em a delimiter, a
space, a control character \(em without quoting gymnastics. A non\-hex digit
after
.B \ex
is an error.
.SS Divergences from bash
.TP
.B Matching is byte\-oriented.
.B .
consumes one byte, not one character, so
.RB \[dq] "é =~ ^.$" \[dq]
is false and
.RB \[dq] "é =~ ^..$" \[dq]
is true.
.BR bash (1)
in a UTF\-8 locale matches whole characters. Literal non\-ASCII text in a
pattern still works, because both sides are the same bytes. This matches
.BR strlen ,
which is likewise a byte count.
.TP
.B POSIX character classes are not supported.
.RB \[dq] [[:alpha:]] \[dq]
is read as an ordinary bracket expression followed by the literal text
.RB \[dq] :] \[dq],
so it
.B silently fails to match
rather than being rejected. Use
.B \ew
or an explicit range. This is the one unsupported construct that does not
announce itself.
.TP
.B No backreferences in a pattern.
.B \e1
is the literal character
.BR 1 ,
so
.RB \[dq] "(a)\e1" \[dq]
matches
.RB \[dq] a1 \[dq],
not
.RB \[dq] aa \[dq].
.RB ( sub
and
.B gsub
do take
.B \e0
\-
.B \e9
in the
.IR replacement ;
that is a different position.)
.TP
.B No lookahead, lookbehind, or non\-capturing groups.
.RB \[dq] (?= \[dq],
.RB \[dq] (?<= \[dq],
and
.RB \[dq] (?: \[dq]
are rejected
.RB ( SB5023 ).
.TP
.B No word boundary.
.B \eb
is the literal character
.BR b .
.TP
.B A literal hyphen in a bracket must be escaped.
Write
.RB \[dq] [ab\e\-] \[dq];
.RB \[dq] [ab\-] \[dq]
is rejected as a malformed range.
.TP
.B No counted repetition of a group.
.RB \[dq] (ab){2} \[dq]
is rejected
.RB ( SB5023 ).
Write
.RB \[dq] (ab)(ab) \[dq]
instead.
.IP
This is a limitation of the engine rather than an oversight. The compiler
expands a bound by re\-lexing the single preceding
.IR atom ;
.RB \[dq] ( \[dq]
and
.RB \[dq] ) \[dq]
are operators, not atoms, so the atom it would repeat is the group's last
element rather than the group. Earlier versions did exactly that:
.RB \[dq] (ab){2} \[dq]
compiled as
.RB \[dq] (ab)b \[dq]
and reported no match on
.RB \[dq] ababababab \[dq],
where bash matches. Rejecting is the deliberate choice \(em a wrong answer
with no diagnostic is worse than a diagnostic. The rejection covers every
position, not just a leading group:
.RB \[dq] a(bc){2} \[dq],
.RB \[dq] (a)(b){2} \[dq]
and
.RB \[dq] (a){2}b \[dq]
are all rejected, while
.RB \[dq] (ab)x{2} \[dq]
is accepted because the repeated atom there is
.BR x .
.TP
.B No empty alternative.
.RB \[dq] (a|) \[dq]
is rejected;
.BR bash (1)
accepts it. Write
.RB \[dq] (a)? \[dq].
.TP
.B No case\-insensitive matching.
The engine has no
.BR REG_ICASE .
Fold both sides instead:
.RB \[dq] "match $(tolower $x) $(tolower $pat)" \[dq].
.SS Interior NUL bytes
A regex operation examines its subject only
.B up to the subject's first interior NUL byte.
Bytes after that are neither matched nor replaced, and are preserved
unchanged. Like the POSIX bracket classes above, this boundary
.B does not announce itself:
the operation reports success and returns a wrong answer.
.PP
This applies to
.RB \[dq] "[[ =~ ]]" \[dq],
.BR match ,
.BR matchall ,
.BR sub ,
.BR gsub ,
and the
.RB \[dq] ${x:s/\[u2026]/\[u2026]/} \[dq]
and
.RB \[dq] "x =~ s/\[u2026]/\[u2026]/" \[dq]
mutation forms. A scalar itself is byte\-transparent \(em
.B strlen
counts the whole string, and NULs survive assignment, pipes, redirects and
command substitution. It is only the regex engine, which is C\-string based,
that stops early.
.PP
In practice a NUL is the
.B record separator
(DD#99), and text within one record does not contain one. If a scalar holds
NULs it is mid\-split, and the operation to reach for is
.B read
or
.BR matchall ,
which work on the fields, not
.RB \[dq] =~ \[dq]
on the joined subject.
.SS Quoting
The pattern is an ordinary argument \(em one word \(em not a specially parsed
field. Quote it whenever it contains characters the shell would otherwise
take: a space, a
.RB \[dq] # \[dq],
or a doubled
.RB \[dq] (( \[dq]
\(em which an unquoted pattern hands to the arithmetic lexer, ending the
pattern there, so
.RB \[dq] "=~ ^((a))$" \[dq]
is rejected
.RB ( SB4014 )
while
.RB \[dq] "=~ '^((a))$'" \[dq]
is fine. The trigger is adjacency, not nesting \(em
.RB \[dq] "^(a(b))$" \[dq]
has the same depth and compiles. A single
.RB \[dq] ( \[dq]
needs no quoting.
.RB \[dq] "=~ '^(a|b)$'" \[dq]
is literal,
.RB \[dq] "=~ \[dq]^$p$\[dq]" \[dq]
interpolates, and
.RB \[dq] "=~ $p" \[dq]
takes the whole pattern from a variable. Single quotes are the usual choice,
since a regex is full of characters that mean something to both languages.
.SS Validation
A pattern that is a compile\-time literal is compiled by the same engine
during compilation, so a malformed one is a
.B compile
error
.RB ( SB5023 ).
A pattern that comes from a variable is compiled at run time; a malformed one
then exits with status 2.
.
.SH SASH-CONVERT
.B sash\-convert
.RB [ \-\-annotate ]
.RB [ \-o
.IR output ]
.RI [ input.sh ]
.PP
Converts a bash script to sash-native source. Reads standard input when no
input file is given.
.TP
.B \-\-annotate
Annotate the output.
.TP
.BI \-o \ output
Write to
.IR output .
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print usage and exit.
.PP
Unlike
.BR sash ,
.B sash\-convert
rejects an unknown flag.
.
.SH BUGS
.SS The \-d / \-f collision
One spelling means three different things depending on where it sits. This
is the confusion this page was written for:
.PP
.RS
.TS
l l l l.
spelling	in read	in mapfile	in [[ ]]
_
\-d	record delimiter	(not a flag)	is a directory
\-f	field separator	key/value separator	is a regular file
\-a	array target	(not a flag)	file exists
\-t	(not a flag)	accepted no-op	rejected (SB5028)
\-n	(not a flag)	(not a flag)	string is non-empty
.TE
.RE
.PP
Choosing
.B \-d
when you meant
.B \-f
fails silently \(em the read stops at the first delimiter and the trailing
targets are left empty, with no diagnostic.
.SS read \-a ignores \-f
With
.BR \-a ,
splitting uses the
.B record delimiter
.RB ( \-d ),
and
.B \-f is silently ignored:
.PP
.RS
.EX
echo "a:b:c" | read -a arr -d:   # 3 elements
echo "a:b:c" | read -a arr -f:   # 1 element; -f discarded
.EE
.RE
.PP
The
.B \-d
behavior is deliberate (it is what makes the
.BR sash\-getopt (1)
NUL-splitting idiom work, spec 22.26/22.27). The silent discard of
.B \-f
is not spec'd. bash splits an array read on
.BR IFS ,
i.e. on the field separator.
.SS Standalone and pipeline read disagree
.B read
is lowered by two different paths, and they do not enforce the same rules:
.PP
.RS
.EX
read -f: x                 # SB5016 (field separator needs several targets)
echo "a:b" | read -f: x    # accepted; x gets the whole line
.EE
.RE
.PP
The same divergence affects the DD#86 acknowledgment
.RB \[dq] :\ $v \[dq],
which is accepted after a pipeline
.B read
but rejected after a standalone one \(em and rejected by the SSA taint
backstop, with no
.B SB
code at all. See
.BR notes/tasks/read\-taint\-backstop\-no\-sb\-code.md .
.SS Unknown flags are handled two different ways
.RS
.TP
.B read
rejects \(em
.BR SB5015 .
.TP
.B mapfile
silently ignores.
.TP
.B sash
(the driver) rejects.
.TP
.B sash\-convert
rejects.
.RE
.PP
.B mapfile
is the remaining outlier. The driver used to ignore an unknown flag too \(em
.B sash --bogus script.sh
compiled and exited 0 \(em so a typo such as
.B \-\-emit\-C
silently produced a different artifact than the one requested. Fixed; see
.BR notes/tasks/cli\-usage\-and\-unknown\-flags.md .
.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Every rejection carries an
.B SB
code. Those referenced here:
.RS
.TP
.B SB5011
command resolution failed (e.g.
.BR readarray ).
.TP
.B SB5095
an absolute command path that is not an executable file (DD#23).
.TP
.B SB5096
a quoted constant command name that is not said the one accepted way \(em
either it needed no quotes at all, or it needs them and was double\-quoted or
concatenated.
.TP
.B SB4074
an empty command name
.RB ( \[aq]\[aq] " or " \[dq]\[dq] ).
.TP
.B SB5015
.BR read :
unknown flag.
.TP
.B SB5016
.BR read \ \-f
with a single target and no array \(em
.B standalone shape only,
see BUGS.
.TP
.B SB5019
numeric operator in
.RB \[dq] [[\ ]] \[dq];
use
.RB \[dq] ((\ )) \[dq].
.TP
.B SB5020
tainted value used without
.BR attest .
.TP
.B SB5050
variable assigned but never read.
.TP
.B SB5056
read of the don't-care binder
.BR _ .
.RE
.
